Connecting the vendor Ethernet driver Realtek (drivers/net/rtl819x).

The driver code itself is not included in the patch: it is an overlay in
target/linux/rtkmipsel/files/drivers/net/rtl819x/. It was assembled from the
vendor's Realtek SDK v3.6.0 source tree.
The patch only has to show the directory to the kernel build system - hence the two lines.

111-rlx_819x_ethernetDriver.patch is arranged in exactly the same way in the openwrt-AC10 fork:
all his code is also in files/, and the patch consists of the same two lines.

The insertion point at Kconfig is selected before `endif # NETDEVICES`, not after
`source "drivers/net/hyperv/Kconfig"`, like AC10: in 4.14 appeared between them
FUJITSU_ES, and binding to hyperv would provide a more fragile context.

The patch was generated from the Linux 4.14.275 reference tree rather than edited by
hand, so its context matches that source revision.

--- a/drivers/net/Kconfig
+++ b/drivers/net/Kconfig
@@ -485,4 +485,6 @@
 
 source "drivers/net/hyperv/Kconfig"
 
+source "drivers/net/rtl819x/Kconfig"
+
 endif # NETDEVICES
--- a/drivers/net/Makefile
+++ b/drivers/net/Makefile
@@ -75,3 +75,4 @@
 obj-$(CONFIG_NTB_NETDEV) += ntb_netdev.o
 
 obj-$(CONFIG_FUJITSU_ES) += fjes/
+obj-$(CONFIG_RTL_819X_SWCORE) += rtl819x/
--- a/drivers/char/Makefile
+++ b/drivers/char/Makefile
@@ -60,3 +60,4 @@
 obj-$(CONFIG_TILE_SROM)		+= tile-srom.o
 obj-$(CONFIG_XILLYBUS)		+= xillybus/
 obj-$(CONFIG_POWERNV_OP_PANEL)	+= powernv-op-panel.o
+obj-$(CONFIG_RTL_8211F_SUPPORT)	+= rtl_nfbi/
